<<<
that is, as a token of good will), the choice should be between the unhyphened forms, since the accent falls on the second syllable; see HyPHENS. Good will is required when the notion is virtuous intent &c., & goodwill is better when it is benevolence &c.
===
that is, as a token of good will), the choice should be between the unhyphened forms, since the accent falls on the second syllable; see ^^HYPHENS^^. *Good will* is required when the notion is virtuous intent &c., & *goodwill* is better when it is benevolence &c.
